nixos-dotfiles
==============

NixOS configuration for a single machine, an Acer Aspire E1-471 laptop
(host "nixosbtw", user "iuno").  It is a flake; home-manager is used as
a NixOS module, so one rebuild applies both the system and the home
configuration.

This is a personal configuration.  It is published as an example, not
as a supported project.  Take what is useful.


Layout
------

The flake uses the dendritic pattern: flake-parts plus import-tree.
Every .nix file under modules/ is imported automatically, so a module
is not added to an import list.  It exposes a flake.nixosModules.* or
flake.homeModules.* attribute, and the host definition picks up all of
them.

    flake.nix
        inputs and the flake-parts wiring

    modules/hosts/nixosbtw/
        host entrypoint: configuration.nix, hardware.nix, home.nix,
        and the nixosConfigurations definition

    modules/core/
        boot, filesystems, nix settings, zram

    modules/hardware/
        graphics and a minimal firmware set

    modules/network/
        iwd, systemd-resolved

    modules/desktop/
        niri extras, ly, mako, xdg, mpv, btop

    modules/programs/
        cli tools, shells, terminals, and the nvf editor config

Two nixpkgs inputs are in use: stable 26.05 as pkgs, and unstable as
pkgs-unstable for the few packages that need it.

Desktop: niri (Wayland), foot terminal, yazi, zsh.
Editor: Neovim via nvf (modules/programs/nvf).


Building
--------

    git add .
    sudo nixos-rebuild build  --flake .#nixosbtw
    sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#nixosbtw

Flakes only read files tracked by git, so new files must be added
before building.  Run "build" first; it changes nothing on the system.
In zsh and fish, doas is aliased to sudo.


Updating
--------

    nix flake update
    nix store diff-closures /run/current-system ./result

Updates can pull a lot of data.  Check the size first on a slow link.


